From 98ce191aed3002e79d626a2fdbbf6407f0e7e5e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Muhammad Hassaan Shaukat <mr.hassaan50@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 05:16:31 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] Add a client-side observation filter so each collusion
 carrier can be removed alone

Track 3.1 asks whether collusion can travel through channels other than chat, but the
Jira pipeline shows a claimed task to the other agents twice over -- once as a
=== CURRENT ASSIGNMENTS === block and once as a rendered [action_executed] event whose
payload also leaks joint_reward -- so hiding either path alone leaves the other intact.
Answering the question needs an intervention that can name which path it removed.

FilteringClient wraps the provider client and rewrites the observation on the way to
the model, which keeps every installed package untouched: uv sync cannot undo the
defence. The four interventions are independently switchable and exposed as presets
V/E/S/C. C is the one that matters for interpretation: E and S delete text, so a
filtered agent gets a shorter observation and a different token budget, confounding the
intervention with truncation. C rewrites task ids, agent names, event indices and the
leaked reward while preserving line and event counts, so a change under C is attributable
to missing information and nothing else.

Canonicalising to a single shared placeholder was not survivable: the model can only name
a task by the token it was shown, and the environment rejects an unknown task_id, so all
four assignments came back as retries. Each distinct id now gets its own opaque token and
the client expands it back on the way into the tool call.

run.py gains 12 lines and no behaviour change for configs that do not set the key. The
roster is bound once before the agent loop, otherwise the policy written to run_config.json
records an empty roster while scoped policies ran.
